PyDoc_STRVAR(__pyx_doc_10engine_pkg_3app_3App_226_clear_viewmodel_depth, "Reset the depth buffer to the far plane so the first-person weapon\n        is never cut by world geometry.\n\n        The view model is a camera-space shell held ~0.6 m in front of the eye;\n        sharing the world depth buffer meant a wall within arm\047s reach sliced\n        the gun in half (only the part in front of the wall survived), and at\n        ~0.2 m the weapon disappeared entirely. Source renders the view model\n        in its own depth pass for exactly this reason.\n\n        moderngl\047s ``clear()`` always wipes colour too, so instead a fullscreen\n        quad writes ``gl_FragDepth = 1.0`` with colour writes masked off: the\n        scene colour is untouched and only the depth range is reset. The weapon\n        then depth-tests against its own (cleared) depth, which keeps it solid\n        and self-occluding while world geometry can no longer erase it.\n\n        The mask has to be set on the *framebuffer* (``Framebuffer.color_mask``)\n        and applied by binding it: ``Context`` has no ``color_mask``, so the old\n        ``ctx.color_mask = ...`` only created a Python attribute and left colour\n        writes enabled. The depth clear then painted the full-screen quad with\n        whatever colour the driver produced - on some GPUs a solid purple fill\n        that stuck to the screen for as long as a weapon was held.\n        ");
